This example demonstrates advanced use of data structures.
Its purpose is to store, interactively, a road map, with names of towns
connected by highways. Each highway section has an id number, and a distance.
The id nubmers can repeat for several sections (as in the real life).

After typing "test", you get into an interactive mode, which accepts
three commands:
   add [town1] [town2] [id] [distance]
   route [town1] [town2]
   exit
Examples:
   add Syracuse Watertown 81 65
   route NewYork Boston
   exit

The command 'add' adds a new highway section to the map.
Command 'route' finds the shortes route between the two towns, and prints
it including complete routing (towns, highway sections) and the total distance.
Command 'exit' not only exits from the program, but also stores the whole
network to disk, so that when you invoke the program again, you can continue
just where you left off before.
If you want to start form scratch, remove the file 'backup' from your directory.
This is the file that contains the data from your last run.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Note the organization of the program. File 'data.h' declares objects and
data organization, file 'data.cc' contains the methods, and file 'test.cc'
contains the main body of the program. Code Farms' code generator needs
only 'data.h' as its input, and generates files 'zzincl.h' and 'zzfunc.c'.
With these files you start debugging, and you do not have to re-run the code
generator, unless you modify your data definition.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The program works with three objects: 
 Root is an auxilliary object, which keeps a hash table for the fast pickup
of names. It also keeps a collection (linked list) of towns required
internally for the algorithm (front of the wave expanding through the network).
 Hwy represents one highway section.
 Town represents one town.

Data organizations used:
 SINGLE_GRAPH - representing the network as a graph, where edges adjacent
                to any node form a singly-linked list
 NAME - variable length names are treated as objects, which simplifies code
 HASH - hash table of towns
 UTILITIES - memory and disk saving utilities

The hash table assumes that you supply two functions:
zz_hashCmp_..() which compares two objects, using your particular key;
zz_hashInd_..() which generates the hashing index.
The first function is trivial. If you don't want anything special, the library
provides a convenient default based on the golden ratio algorithm:
ZZhashStr() for hashing a string, and ZZhashInt() for hashing an integer.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Paper "The secret of efficient software design: Internal data organization",
by Jiri Soukup, has been submitted to the IEEE
Electro-International Conference, May 12-14 1992, Boston, and copies are
available from Code Farms. The paper describes this problem in more
detail, and explains both the data structures and the algorithm.
